  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is nursing a good career"</h2>

  <p>
    Nursing is a real, regulated, structurally in-demand clinical career in India, not a dressed-up "helping
    people" job with no ceiling. But "is nursing good" and "will the first few years feel good on your body and
    schedule" are two different questions, and most course brochures blur them together until you cannot tell
    which one is actually being answered.
  </p>

  <p>
    The honest split is this: the daily reality — rotating shifts, patient handling, documentation load, and
    modest starting pay in most private roles — is genuinely demanding and not exaggerated. The payoff on the
    other side — government-scale pay, specialisation income, and a real abroad income jump — is also genuinely
    real, but it belongs to people who plan the route deliberately and treat the early grind as clinical
    skill-building, not a personal failure.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not the "nursing is a noble calling, join now" pitch flooding admission material, and it is not a
      warning to avoid healthcare careers entirely either. Both miss the point. The field genuinely rewards people
      who go in with accurate expectations about the shift work and pay curve and build real specialisation — and
      it genuinely wears down people who went in on the "always in demand" idea alone, without checking what that
      demand is actually compensating for.
    </p>
    <p>
      One more thing this decision is not: something you can test through a short online course. Nursing is a
      formally regulated clinical profession in every entry route — the legal minimum is an ANM, GNM, or BSc
      Nursing qualification from a recognised institution, a compulsory supervised internship, and registration
      with your State Nursing Council before you can practise or use the title. There is no self-taught shortcut
      into this field, so the "is it good" question has to be answered before you commit years and real fees, not
      after.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="entry-routes">ANM vs GNM vs BSc: which entry route actually matters</h2>

  <p>
    Most "is nursing a good career" articles only talk about a BSc Nursing graduate's path. That skips the first
    real decision most Indian nursing aspirants actually face: which of the three legal entry routes to take, and
    why that choice shapes your ceiling for a decade or more.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Eligibility</th>
          <th>Duration</th>
          <th>Real scope and ceiling</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {entryRouteRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Route"><strong>{row.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Eligibility">{row.eligibility}</td>
            <td data-label="Duration">{row.duration}</td>
            <td data-label="Real scope and ceiling">{row.scope} {row.ceiling}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      None of the three routes is automatically "better." A GNM diploma gets you into hospital staff-nurse work
      almost as fast as a BSc, for less time and money — a genuinely strong option if you need to start earning
      sooner. A BSc costs more time and money upfront but is the most direct gate into M.Sc Nursing, teaching
      roles, and the smoothest paperwork route for most international licensing pathways later.
    </p>
    <p>
      Registration with your State Nursing Council is mandatory for all three routes before you can legally
      practise, and India's nursing regulation is mid-transition from the Indian Nursing Council toward the
      National Nursing and Midwifery Commission — verify the current process on your state council's website
      directly rather than assuming last year's steps still apply.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="pay-reality">The real pay, not the brochure number</h2>

  <p>
    "Nursing salary in India" gets quoted as a single flattering number far too often. The real spread between a
    private-hospital fresher and a government-placed, specialised senior nurse is large, and which side of that
    spread you land on depends more on employer type than on which entry route you took.
  </p>

