      None of this means avoid nursing. It means go in aware that the profession which cares for patients through
      illness and injury also carries real occupational risk of its own — and build the sleep, recovery, and
      safety habits from year one that reduce that risk, rather than learning them the hard way after a first bad
      shift or a first needlestick incident.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ai-risk">Will AI replace nursing jobs</h2>

  <p>
    This is the question underneath every "is nursing a good career" search once someone gets past the salary
    numbers. The honest answer is not a flat yes or no — it depends on which half of the job you actually do.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
