      <ul>
        {aiComparison.teal.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Healthcare workforce research consistently finds that the human-trust layer — explaining a diagnosis,
      calming a frightened patient, catching a subtle sign that something is wrong before a monitor flags it — is
      exactly the part of nursing that automation cannot replicate. The pressure lands almost entirely on routine,
      scriptable tasks: documentation, basic monitoring, and first-draft patient-education content. It does not
      land on hands-on care or the clinical judgment a nurse is actually trained and paid to exercise.
    </p>
    <p>
      The safer long-term move is not avoiding these tools — it is pairing a clinical specialisation with comfort
      using digital documentation, monitoring dashboards, and AI-assisted charting, since that combination is
      already where hospital-chain and abroad hiring is growing fastest.
    </p>
  </div>

